ed to evaluate against criteria: reliability, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility.\n\nWe should perhaps start with a candidate that seems strong overall. Let's examine each:\n\nCandidate A: can anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: not open to new ideas, eats unhealthily. Leadership good, anticipation good for safety. Not open to new ideas could affect adaptability. Eating unhealthily maybe health risk but not directly criteria. So strengths: operational reliability? Anticipation suggests good situational awareness. Technical competence: sees complex connections, spatial vision. Calmness? Not known. Cooperation? Leadership maybe good, but not open to new ideas may affect crew cooperation. Responsibility? Leadership. So mixed.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Concerns: makes nasty remarks, sometimes adopts wrong tone. So reliability high, stress resilience high, technical competence (computer skills, weather assessment). But cooperation issues (nasty remarks, tone). Could be problematic for crew.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education, understands complicated technology, excellent attention skills. Strengths: decision quality, technical competence, attention. Concerns: communication, egocentric, not willing to develop. Cooperation issues.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, has hot temper, regarded as loner. Strengths: adaptability, concentration, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: hot temper, loner (cooperation). So cooperation issues.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some cooperation concerns. B has nasty remarks, tone issues. C has communication difficulty, egocentric. D has hot temper, loner. A has not open to new ideas, maybe less adaptable. Which is least concerning? B's nasty remarks could be serious for crew. D's hot temper also serious. C's communication difficulty may affect safety. A's not open to new ideas may affect adaptability but maybe less severe. So A might be best overall despite not open to new ideas.\n\nThus I could argue for Candidate A as current recommendation, citing strengths and noting concerns but weighing them.\n\nWe must reference existing discussion (none) but can say \"Given we have not yet discussed any candidate, I propose we start with Candidate A\". That is okay.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: \"Candidate A can anticipate dangerous situations\" and \"Candidate A has very good leadership qualities\". Also mention \"not open to new ideas\". Explain implications.\n\nVote for Candidate A.\n\nNow produce output."}
